A Formal Framework for Safety-Constrained Adaptive Hurdle Selection in Multiplanetary Food Systems

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Date: August 30, 2026

AI : ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek, Kimi AI

Abstract

The transition to a multiplanetary civilization requires autonomous food systems capable of transforming heterogeneous biological resources into safe, nutritious, and shelf-stable products under severe resource constraints. While the combination of preservation hurdles is well-established in food science, the real-time, per-batch adaptive selection and control of such hurdles under uncertainty remains an open problem. This thesis develops a rigorous mathematical foundation for the decision layer of the Universal Food Preservation and Reconstruction Architecture, focusing on formal guarantees for microbial safety, nutrient retention, and resource optimality.

The main contributions are: (i) a stochastic hybrid model that includes processing and subsequent storage dynamics, with explicit incorporation of microbial growth during shelf life and certified polynomial approximations of non-polynomial kinetics; (ii) a corrected stochastic barrier certificate framework for probabilistic safety verification, including explicit reset conditions for hybrid transitions; (iii) a robust tube-based model predictive control scheme with recursive feasibility based on a robust invariant terminal set, separating hard microbial safety constraints from soft nutrient performance constraints; (iv) a submodular sensor selection framework restricted to a linear-Gaussian setting where submodularity is provable, with extensions to nonlinear systems treated as principled heuristics; (v) a digital twin validation method using sequential conformal prediction adapted to dynamical systems with mode switches; and (vi) a formalization of the Food Compiler as a mapping from specifications to certified adaptive control policies, with the novelty explicitly framed as adaptive hurdle selection under uncertainty.

The proposed framework is theoretically sound, addresses key limitations of existing literature, and lays the groundwork for autonomous, safe, and resource-efficient food manufacturing in space habitats.

Keywords: Multi-hurdle preservation, stochastic hybrid systems, barrier certificates, robust model predictive control, cyber-physical systems, space food systems, formal verification, digital twin.

