3. Food Processing and Storage Stochastic Hybrid Model

We model preservation of a generic moist food product with three main hurdles, thermal, acid, and desiccation, and a subsequent storage phase. The state vector is

x = [T,\; pH,\; a_w,\; M,\; N,\; \theta]^\top,

where M denotes \log_{10} microbial load. The safety constraint is imposed on M, while nutrient retention is tracked via N.

3.1 Processing Modes

Heating mode. The dynamics are:

\begin{aligned}
dT &= \alpha (T_{\text{set}} - T) dt + \sigma_T dW_T, \\
dM &= -\frac{k_{\text{therm}}(T, pH, a_w)}{\ln 10} dt + \sigma_M dW_M, \\
dN &= -k_{\text{deg}}(T, pH, a_w) N dt,
\end{aligned}

where k_{\text{therm}} = k_{\text{ref}} \cdot 10^{(T - T_{\text{ref}})/z_T} \cdot f(pH, a_w) is the Bigelow inactivation rate, expressed in \log_{10} reduction per unit time. The term \sigma_M dW_M represents intrinsic stochasticity in microbial inactivation due to population heterogeneity. The nutrient degradation rate k_{\text{deg}} is typically modeled by Arrhenius kinetics.

Acidification mode. The dynamics are:

\begin{aligned}
d\,pH &= -\beta (pH - pH_{\text{target}}) dt + \sigma_{pH} dW_{pH}, \\
dM &= -\frac{k_{\text{acid}}(pH)}{\ln 10} dt, \\
dN &= -k_{\text{acid\_deg}}(pH) N dt.
\end{aligned}

Acidification primarily inhibits growth rather than causing rapid inactivation; the inactivation term k_{\text{acid}} is small but nonzero.

Drying mode. The dynamics are:

\begin{aligned}
d a_w &= -\eta (a_w - a_{w,\text{target}}) dt + \sigma_{a_w} dW_{a_w}, \\
dM &= -\frac{k_{\text{dry}}(a_w, T)}{\ln 10} dt, \\
dN &= -k_{\text{dry\_deg}}(a_w, T) N dt.
\end{aligned}

Water activity is related to moisture content via a food-specific sorption isotherm:

a_w = f_{\text{sorption}}(\text{moisture}, T, \text{food type}).

This relationship must be incorporated into the drying mode dynamics to ensure physical consistency.

Transitions between modes are triggered by guards, such as target temperature, pH, or water activity reached. Kinetic parameters are bounded random variables with known distributions from predictive microbiology literature.

3.2 Storage Mode

After packaging, the product enters a storage mode with dynamics:

\begin{aligned}
dM &= \frac{\mu_{\max}(a_w, T, pH)}{\ln 10} \cdot \left(1 - \frac{10^{M}}{10^{M_{\max}}}\right) dt + \sigma_{M,\text{storage}} dW_M, \\
dN &= -k_{\text{storage\_deg}}(a_w, T) N dt, \\
dT &= \sigma_{T,\text{storage}} dW_T, \\
d a_w &= \sigma_{a_w,\text{storage}} dW_{a_w},
\end{aligned}

where \mu_{\max} is the maximum specific growth rate in \log_{10} units per unit time. The logistic growth term is written in terms of actual concentration, hence the conversion 10^M. The diffusion terms model environmental fluctuations during long-term storage. If a_w is below a critical threshold, \mu_{\max} \approx 0, and the product is microbially stable. The safety constraint must hold throughout processing and storage up to the shelf life T_{\text{shelf}}.

3.3 Non-Polynomial Kinetics and Certified Approximation

The Bigelow inactivation and growth models are non-polynomial functions of temperature, pH, and water activity. To apply sum-of-squares-based verification, we construct polynomial approximations \tilde{k}(x) of the true rate functions k(x) over the operating region \mathcal{X} using Taylor models or Chebyshev interpolation, with rigorous error bounds \Delta k such that

|k(x) - \tilde{k}(x)| \le \Delta k \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{X}.

These error bounds are computed via interval arithmetic or Bernstein coefficients. The approximation error enters the microbial dynamics multiplicatively:

dM = -\frac{\tilde{k}(x)}{\ln 10} dt + e(x) dt + \sigma_M dW_M,

where

e(x) = -\frac{k(x) - \tilde{k}(x)}{\ln 10}.

The effective disturbance is state-dependent and bounded by

|e(x)| \le \frac{\Delta k}{\ln 10}.

Barrier certificates and robust controllers are then designed for the approximated system with this bounded disturbance, guaranteeing safety for all possible approximation errors.

3.4 Digital Twin Model

For simulation and controller synthesis, we use a discrete-time approximation:

x_{k+1} = F(x_k, u_k, w_k),

where w_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma) captures process noise and model mismatch. The mapping F is obtained by discretizing the stochastic differential equations using the Euler–Maruyama scheme. For robust MPC, we assume w_k lies in a bounded set \mathcal{W} with probability at least 1-\varepsilon_{\mathcal{W}}, constructed using the certified error bounds and the Gaussian noise distribution.

Assumption 3.1. The stochastic process is Markov, noise terms are independent across modes, and drift and diffusion functions are locally Lipschitz. All model parameters lie within known bounded intervals. The state space \mathcal{X} is compact.

