4. Probabilistic Safety Verification via Barrier Certificates

We focus on the microbial safety constraint: M(t) \le M_{\text{safe}} for all t \in [0, T_{\text{total}}], where T_{\text{total}} includes processing and storage. The unsafe set is

X_u = \{ x \in X : M > M_{\text{safe}} \}.

Given a candidate policy \pi, possibly a feedback law u = \kappa(x), we aim to find a barrier certificate B_q(x) separating X_0 from X_u, using the corrected hybrid definition.

4.1 Correct Barrier Certificate Theorem

Theorem 4.1 (Stochastic Safety via Nonnegative Supermartingale).
Suppose \mathcal{X} is compact and there exists a family of twice differentiable functions B_q: X \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} and constants c, \eta > 0 satisfying Definition 2.2 for the closed-loop system under policy \pi. Then for any initial state x(0) \in X_0,

\Pr\left( \exists t \in [0,T]: x(t) \in X_u \right) \le \frac{c}{\eta}.

If B_q(x) \le c on X_0 and B_q(x) \ge \eta on X_u for all modes q, the probability bound is independent of the time horizon T.

Proof. Since \mathcal{L}_q B_q \le 0 in each mode and B_{q'}(\text{Reset}(q,q',x)) \le B_q(x) across transitions, the process B_{q(t)}(x_t) is a nonnegative supermartingale. By Doob's maximal inequality for nonnegative supermartingales,

\Pr\left( \sup_{t \le T} B_{q(t)}(x_t) \ge \eta \right) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[B_{q(0)}(x_0)]}{\eta} \le \frac{c}{\eta}.

If x(t) \in X_u, then B_{q(t)}(x(t)) \ge \eta, so the event \{\exists t: x(t) \in X_u\} is contained in \{\sup_t B_{q(t)}(x_t) \ge \eta\}. The result follows. \square

To achieve safety level \varepsilon, we require c/\eta \le \varepsilon. For a given barrier, this dictates the scaling. In practice, we search for B_q and constants satisfying this inequality.

Remark 4.1 (Finite-Horizon Refinement). For finite-time horizons, tighter bounds can be obtained using time-varying barrier certificates B_q(t,x) that satisfy

\frac{\partial B_q}{\partial t} + \mathcal{L}_q B_q \le 0.

This formulation often yields less conservative results than time-invariant barriers at the cost of increased computational complexity.

4.2 SOS Synthesis with Certified Error Bounds

Assume the polynomial approximation of the dynamics is used, and the approximation error is included as a bounded disturbance e \in [-\Delta k/\ln 10, \Delta k/\ln 10]. We seek barriers B_q such that for all x \in \text{Inv}(q), e \in \mathcal{E},

\mathcal{L}_{\tilde{f}_q + e} B_q(x) \le 0,

where \tilde{f}_q is the polynomial drift. This robust condition can be enforced via SOS programming with the S-procedure to handle the disturbance set \mathcal{E}. The constraints become:

-B_q(x) \in \Sigma[x] \quad \text{on } X_0 \cap \text{Inv}(q),

B_q(x) - \eta \in \Sigma[x] \quad \text{on } X_u \cap \text{Inv}(q),

-\mathcal{L}_{\tilde{f}_q}B_q(x) - \sum_i \lambda_i(x) (e_i^2 - \Delta_i^2) \in \Sigma[x,e],

B_q(x) - B_{q'}(\text{Reset}(q,q',x)) \in \Sigma[x] \quad \text{on } \text{Guard}(q,q'),

for appropriate multipliers \lambda_i \in \Sigma[x,e]. The resulting optimization is a semidefinite program.

4.3 Computational Tractability

The barrier synthesis is performed offline, once per policy specification. The online controller only evaluates B_q(x) and checks \mathcal{L}_q B_q(x) \le 0 at the current state, which is a simple polynomial evaluation. For the safety-critical subspace (T, pH, a_w, M) of dimension 4, SOS barrier synthesis with degree-4 polynomials is computationally feasible. The full state including nutrients is handled by a separate observer, as discussed in Section 8.

