6. Sensor Selection via Submodular Optimization

The quality of state estimation affects MPC performance. We formulate sensor selection as a combinatorial optimization problem, but recognize that submodularity of the value function is not generally guaranteed for nonlinear systems. Therefore, we restrict the theoretical guarantee to a linear-Gaussian approximation of the system, where submodularity can be proven.

6.1 Linear-Gaussian Setting

Assume the system can be approximated by a linear time-invariant model:

x_{k+1} = A x_k + B u_k + w_k, \quad w_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q),

with measurements from sensor set S:

y_k = C(S) x_k + v_k, \quad v_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R(S)).

The optimal state estimate is given by a Kalman filter, and the steady-state error covariance P(S) satisfies the algebraic Riccati equation. For a quadratic cost with weighting matrix W \succeq 0, the optimal infinite-horizon LQG cost V(S) is a known function of P(S).

Theorem 6.1 (Submodularity of LQG Cost).
Assume the system is stable and detectable for all sensor sets S. Then the function V(S) = \mathrm{Tr}(W P(S)) is monotone non-increasing and submodular in the sensor set S.

Proof. This result follows from the concavity of the Riccati operator in the information matrix; see Gupta et al. (2006) for a detailed proof. \square

Corollary 6.2. The greedy algorithm that iteratively adds the sensor with the largest marginal gain achieves a (1-1/e)-approximation to the optimal sensor set of size at most K.

For the nonlinear food system, we propose to use the linearized model around a nominal trajectory to select sensors, then validate the choice via Monte Carlo simulation of the full nonlinear closed-loop system. The submodularity guarantee is thus only approximate for the true system, but provides a principled heuristic with a posteriori validation.

6.2 Observability Analysis

For the selected sensor suite \{NIR, pH, a_w, T, ATP\}, we must ensure observability of the safety-critical subspace (T, pH, a_w, M). For the linearized system, compute the observability matrix

\mathcal{O}(C, A) = \begin{bmatrix} C \\ CA \\ \vdots \\ CA^{n-1} \end{bmatrix}

and verify it has full rank. For the nonlinear system, local weak observability can be checked via the Lie derivative rank condition.

