7. Digital Twin Validation with Sequential Conformal Prediction

The digital twin is a simulation model used for prediction. Standard conformal prediction assumes exchangeable data, which fails for time-series from controlled processes. We therefore adopt sequential conformal prediction adapted to dynamical systems with mode switches.

7.1 Sequential Conformal Prediction for Dynamical Systems

Assume we have a sequence of residuals r_t = \| x_{t+1} - F_{\text{low}}(x_t, u_t) \| from the digital twin. For a dynamical system, these residuals are serially correlated and may exhibit distribution shifts at mode transitions. The adaptive conformal inference method of Gibbs and Candès maintains a dynamically adjusted threshold \hat{q}_t such that

\Pr\left( \frac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{1}_{r_t > \hat{q}_t} \le \alpha + o(1) \right) \to 1

under bounded variation in the residual sequence. This provides a long-run coverage guarantee without exchangeability, suitable for online operation.

Theorem 7.1 (Sequential Coverage Guarantee).
Under bounded cumulative variation in the residual sequence, the adaptive conformal inference method ensures that the empirical miscoverage rate converges to the target \alpha almost surely.

For systems with explicit mode switches, such as the heating-to-acidification transition, the residual distribution may shift abruptly. In this case, mode-aware conformal prediction should be used, where separate thresholds are maintained for each mode q, and the appropriate threshold is applied based on the current mode.

Theorem 7.2 (Mode-Aware Coverage Guarantee).
If the residual sequence is exchangeable within each mode and transitions are detectable, mode-aware conformal prediction maintains coverage \alpha within each mode individually.

The scalar residual bound \hat{q}_t is converted into a multivariate disturbance set for the MPC by taking a conservative bound, such as

\mathcal{W}_t = \{ w : \|w\|_\infty \le \gamma \hat{q}_t \},

where \gamma \ge 1 accounts for the multivariate nature of the residuals and the conversion from scalar to vector bounds.

7.2 Integration with Robust MPC

We integrate the conformal prediction bound into the robust MPC by enlarging the disturbance set \mathcal{W} to include prediction errors up to the current \hat{q}_t. This ensures that digital twin inaccuracies do not compromise safety. The enlargement must be performed conservatively, accounting for multi-step prediction error accumulation.

