8. Formalization of the Food Compiler

The Food Compiler takes high-level specifications and produces an executable, adaptive processing policy. Unlike prior hurdle technology, the policy is not a fixed recipe but a feedback law that chooses hurdles based on real-time sensor data.

Definition 8.1 (Specification)

A specification is a tuple

\mathcal{S} = (N_{\text{target}}, T_{\text{shelf}}, R_{\text{max}}, Q_{\text{sensory}}),

where N_{\text{target}} is the target nutrient retention vector, T_{\text{shelf}} the required shelf life, R_{\text{max}} the maximum resource budget, and Q_{\text{sensory}} sensory quality constraints.

Definition 8.2 (Adaptive Processing Policy)

An adaptive policy \pi is a mapping from the current state estimate \hat{x}_t to control actions u_t, along with a terminal packaging decision. It may switch between processing modes based on observed state.

Definition 8.3 (Food Compiler)

The Food Compiler is a function

\mathcal{C}: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{B} \to (\pi, B, \mathcal{X}_f, \mathcal{W}),

where \mathcal{B} is the set of possible biomass inputs, \pi is an adaptive policy, B is a barrier certificate, \mathcal{X}_f is a robust invariant terminal set, and \mathcal{W} is a calibrated disturbance set. The output includes not just the policy but also its safety certificate, which is the actual mathematical contribution.

Theorem 8.1 (Compiler Correctness).
Given a valid stochastic hybrid model, a feasible specification \mathcal{S}, and a biomass input b whose state lies within the designed operating region, the Food Compiler returns a policy \pi and a barrier certificate B such that the closed-loop system satisfies the safety requirement

\Pr\left( \exists t \in [0, T_{\text{shelf}}]: M(t) > M_{\text{safe}} \right) \le \frac{c}{\eta} \le \varepsilon

and the nutrient retention constraint

\Pr\left( N(t) \ge N_{\min} \ \forall t \in [0, T_{\text{shelf}}] \right) \ge 1-\varepsilon_N.

Proof. By construction, the compiler uses the verified tube MPC controller with the barrier certificate and robust terminal set, inheriting their guarantees via Theorems 4.1 and 5.1. The barrier certificate provides the microbial safety probability bound, while the tube MPC ensures recursive feasibility and hard constraint satisfaction for the nominal trajectory. The nutrient constraint is enforced via chance-constrained programming. \square

Novelty statement. The key difference from existing hurdle technology is that the compiler produces adaptive policies that select hurdles per batch based on real-time sensor characterization of heterogeneous inputs, rather than relying on fixed, pre-validated hurdle combinations. This adaptation is formalized as a feedback policy with safety certificates, which has not been previously achieved. The innovation is not the combination of hurdles, but the formal, safety-constrained, adaptive selection and control of hurdles under uncertainty.

