Appendix A: Mathematical Background and Technical Lemmas

A.1 Stochastic Processes and Supermartingales

Definition A.1 (Supermartingale). Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t\ge 0}, \Pr) be a filtered probability space. A stochastic process \{Y_t\}_{t\ge 0} adapted to \{\mathcal{F}_t\} is a supermartingale if \mathbb{E}[|Y_t|] < \infty for all t and

\mathbb{E}[Y_t \mid \mathcal{F}_s] \le Y_s \quad \forall s \le t.

Theorem A.1 (Doob's Maximal Inequality for Nonnegative Supermartingales). Let \{Y_t\}_{t\ge 0} be a right-continuous nonnegative supermartingale. Then for any \eta > 0,

\Pr\left( \sup_{t \ge 0} Y_t \ge \eta \right) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[Y_0]}{\eta}.

Proof. See standard texts on stochastic processes, e.g., Karatzas and Shreve (1991), Theorem 1.3.8. \square

Theorem A.2 (Doob's Maximal Inequality for Finite Horizon). Let \{Y_t\}_{t \in [0,T]} be a right-continuous nonnegative supermartingale. Then for any \eta > 0,

\Pr\left( \sup_{t \in [0,T]} Y_t \ge \eta \right) \le \frac{\mathbb{E}[Y_0]}{\eta}.

Proof. The finite horizon result follows directly from the infinite horizon version by considering the stopped process Y_{t \wedge T}. \square

A.2 Sum-of-Squares Programming

Definition A.2 (Sum-of-Squares Polynomial). A polynomial p(x) \in \mathbb{R}[x] of degree 2d is sum-of-squares (SOS) if there exist polynomials q_i(x) of degree at most d such that

p(x) = \sum_{i=1}^r q_i(x)^2.

Theorem A.3 (SOS and SDP Equivalence). A polynomial p(x) of degree 2d in n variables is SOS if and only if there exists a positive semidefinite matrix Q \succeq 0 such that

p(x) = z(x)^\top Q z(x),

where z(x) is the vector of all monomials of degree at most d.

Proof. See Parrilo (2003) for the constructive proof. The key insight is that p(x) = z(x)^\top Q z(x) with Q \succeq 0 is equivalent to Q = \sum_i q_i q_i^\top for some vectors q_i, which yields p(x) = \sum_i (q_i^\top z(x))^2. \square

Corollary A.4. Checking whether a polynomial is SOS is equivalent to solving a semidefinite programming feasibility problem.

A.3 The S-Procedure

Lemma A.5 (S-Procedure for Quadratic Forms). Let f_0, f_1, \ldots, f_m: \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} be quadratic forms. If there exist \tau_i \ge 0 such that

f_0(x) - \sum_{i=1}^m \tau_i f_i(x) \succeq 0 \quad \forall x,

then

f_0(x) \ge 0 \quad \forall x: f_i(x) \ge 0, \ i = 1, \ldots, m.

Proof. If f_i(x) \ge 0 for all i, then \sum_i \tau_i f_i(x) \ge 0 since \tau_i \ge 0. Therefore f_0(x) \ge \sum_i \tau_i f_i(x) \ge 0. \square

Remark A.1. The S-procedure is conservative in general: there may exist f_0 satisfying the implication without the existence of \tau_i \ge 0. However, for a single quadratic constraint (m=1), the S-procedure is exact.

A.4 Matrix Lemmas

Lemma A.6 (Schur Complement). Let M = \begin{bmatrix} A & B \\ B^\top & C \end{bmatrix} be a symmetric matrix with C \succ 0. Then M \succeq 0 if and only if A - B C^{-1} B^\top \succeq 0.

Proof. Standard result in linear algebra. \square

Lemma A.7 (Matrix Inversion Lemma). For matrices A, B, C, D of compatible dimensions with A and C invertible,

(A + B C D)^{-1} = A^{-1} - A^{-1} B (C^{-1} + D A^{-1} B)^{-1} D A^{-1}.

Proof. Verify by direct multiplication. \square

A.5 Gaussian Concentration Inequalities

Lemma A.8 (Gaussian Tail Bound). Let X \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2). Then for any t > 0,

\Pr(|X| \ge t) \le 2 \exp\left(-\frac{t^2}{2\sigma^2}\right).

Proof. Standard result. \square

Lemma A.9 (Multivariate Gaussian Tail Bound). Let X \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma) in \mathbb{R}^n with \Sigma \succ 0. Then for any t > 0,

\Pr(\|X\|_{\Sigma^{-1}} \ge t) \le \exp\left(-\frac{t^2}{2} + \frac{n}{2}\log 2\right),

where \|X\|_{\Sigma^{-1}} = \sqrt{X^\top \Sigma^{-1} X}.

Proof. The squared Mahalanobis distance follows a chi-squared distribution with n degrees of freedom. \square

