Appendix H: Additional Lemmas and Technical Results

H.1 Lemma on Polynomial Approximation Error Bounds

Lemma H.1 (Bernstein Coefficient Error Bound). Let f: [a, b]^n \to \mathbb{R} be a C^{d+1} function, and let p(x) be the degree-d Bernstein polynomial approximation of f on [a, b]^n. Then for all x \in [a, b]^n,

|f(x) - p(x)| \le \frac{1}{(d+1)!} \sum_{|\alpha| = d+1} \binom{d+1}{\alpha} \max_{x \in [a,b]^n} |D^\alpha f(x)| \cdot \prod_{i=1}^n (b_i - a_i)^{\alpha_i}.

Proof. This is a standard result in approximation theory; see Lorentz (1986). \square

H.2 Lemma on Robust Positive Invariance

Lemma H.2 (Characterization of Robust Positive Invariance). A set \mathcal{X}_f \subseteq \mathcal{X} is robust positively invariant under policy \kappa_f with disturbance set \mathcal{W} if and only if:

F(x, \kappa_f(x), w) \in \mathcal{X}_f \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{X}_f, \ w \in \mathcal{W}.

Proof. This follows directly from the definition of robust positive invariance. \square

Lemma H.3 (Sufficient Condition for Robust Invariance via SOS). Suppose there exists a function V_f: \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} and a policy \kappa_f such that:

V_f(F(x, \kappa_f(x), w)) - V_f(x) \le 0 \quad \forall x \in \mathcal{X}, \ w \in \mathcal{W}.

Then for any \alpha > 0, the sublevel set \mathcal{X}_f = \{ x : V_f(x) \le \alpha \} is robust positively invariant under \kappa_f.

Proof. If x \in \mathcal{X}_f, then V_f(x) \le \alpha. The condition V_f(F(x, \kappa_f(x), w)) \le V_f(x) \le \alpha implies F(x, \kappa_f(x), w) \in \mathcal{X}_f. \square

H.3 Lemma on Kalman Filter Information Form

Lemma H.4 (Information Form of the Kalman Filter). For the linear system:

x_{k+1} = A x_k + B u_k + w_k, \quad w_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q),

with measurements:

y_k = C x_k + v_k, \quad v_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R),

the steady-state information matrix \Phi = P^{-1} satisfies:

\Phi = A^{-\top} \Phi A^{-1} + C^\top R^{-1} C - A^{-\top} \Phi A^{-1} (Q^{-1} + A^{-\top} \Phi A^{-1})^{-1} A^{-\top} \Phi A^{-1}.

Proof. This follows from the matrix inversion lemma applied to the algebraic Riccati equation. \square

