Appendix J: Detailed Proof of the Greedy Approximation Guarantee

Theorem J.1 (Nemhauser–Wolsey–Fisher). Let f: 2^S \to \mathbb{R} be a monotone non-decreasing, submodular set function with f(\emptyset) = 0. Let S_{\text{greedy}} be the set obtained by the greedy algorithm after K iterations, and let S_{\text{opt}} be the optimal set of size K. Then:

f(S_{\text{greedy}}) \ge \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) f(S_{\text{opt}}).

Proof.

Step 1: Notation. Let S_i = \{s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_i\} be the set after i iterations of the greedy algorithm, with s_i being the element added at iteration i. Let S^* = S_{\text{opt}} be the optimal set of size K. Let \Delta_i = f(S_i) - f(S_{i-1}) be the marginal gain at iteration i.

Step 2: Lower bound on marginal gains.

For any i, by the submodularity of f:

f(S^*) \le f(S_i) + \sum_{s \in S^* \setminus S_i} [f(S_i \cup \{s\}) - f(S_i)].

The greedy algorithm selects s_{i+1} to maximize the marginal gain, so for all s \in S \setminus S_i:

f(S_i \cup \{s\}) - f(S_i) \le f(S_i \cup \{s_{i+1}\}) - f(S_i) = \Delta_{i+1}.

Therefore:

f(S^*) \le f(S_i) + |S^* \setminus S_i| \cdot \Delta_{i+1} \le f(S_i) + K \cdot \Delta_{i+1}.

Rearranging:

\Delta_{i+1} \ge \frac{f(S^*) - f(S_i)}{K}.

Step 3: Recursion.

Define \delta_i = f(S^*) - f(S_i). Then:

\delta_{i+1} = \delta_i - \Delta_{i+1} \le \delta_i - \frac{\delta_i}{K} = \delta_i \left(1 - \frac{1}{K}\right).

By induction:

\delta_i \le \delta_0 \left(1 - \frac{1}{K}\right)^i = f(S^*) \left(1 - \frac{1}{K}\right)^i.

Step 4: Final bound.

After K iterations:

f(S^*) - f(S_K) = \delta_K \le f(S^*) \left(1 - \frac{1}{K}\right)^K \le f(S^*) \cdot \frac{1}{e},

where the last inequality uses (1 - 1/K)^K \le e^{-1} for all K \ge 1. Therefore:

f(S_K) \ge f(S^*) \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) = \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) f(S_{\text{opt}}).

This completes the proof. \square

Corollary J.2. For the LQG cost V(S) which is monotone non-increasing and submodular, define f(S) = V(\emptyset) - V(S). Then f is monotone non-decreasing, submodular, and f(\emptyset) = 0. The greedy algorithm applied to f yields:

V(\emptyset) - V(S_{\text{greedy}}) \ge \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) (V(\emptyset) - V(S_{\text{opt}})).

Rearranging:

V(S_{\text{greedy}}) \le \left(1 - \frac{1}{e}\right) V(S_{\text{opt}}) + \frac{1}{e} V(\emptyset).

This is the approximation guarantee stated in Theorem C.1. \square

