Appendix K: Detailed Conformal Prediction Theory

K.1 Standard Split Conformal Prediction

Setup. Let (X_i, Y_i)_{i=1}^n be exchangeable data points, and let (X_{n+1}, Y_{n+1}) be a test point. Let \hat{\mu}(X) be a prediction model, and define residuals:

R_i = |Y_i - \hat{\mu}(X_i)|, \quad i = 1, \ldots, n.

For a target miscoverage rate \alpha, compute the 1-\alpha quantile of the residuals:

\hat{q} = \text{Quantile}_{1-\alpha} \left( \frac{1}{n+1} \sum_{i=1}^n \delta_{R_i} + \delta_{\infty} \right),

where \delta denotes point masses. The prediction interval is:

\hat{C}(X_{n+1}) = \{ y : |y - \hat{\mu}(X_{n+1})| \le \hat{q} \}.

Theorem K.1 (Conformal Coverage Guarantee). Under the exchangeability assumption:

\Pr(Y_{n+1} \in \hat{C}(X_{n+1})) \ge 1-\alpha.

Proof. See Vovk, Gammerman, and Shafer (2005), Theorem 1.2. \square

K.2 Adaptive Conformal Inference

For non-exchangeable data, Gibbs and Candès (2021) propose the adaptive conformal inference method. The threshold is updated online:

\hat{q}_{t+1} = \hat{q}_t + \eta (\text{err}_t - \alpha),

where:

\text{err}_t = \begin{cases} 1 & \text{if } R_t > \hat{q}_t, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise}. \end{cases}

Theorem K.2 (Adaptive Coverage Guarantee). Under bounded variation in the residual distribution, the adaptive conformal inference method maintains long-run coverage:

\frac{1}{T} \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbf{1}_{R_t > \hat{q}_t} \le \alpha + O(T^{-1/2}).

Proof. See Gibbs and Candès (2021), Theorem 3.1. \square

