Appendix L: Detailed Observability Analysis

L.1 Linear Observability

Consider the linearized system around a nominal trajectory:

\delta x_{k+1} = A \delta x_k + B \delta u_k,

\delta y_k = C(S) \delta x_k.

The observability matrix is:

\mathcal{O}(C, A) = \begin{bmatrix} C \\ CA \\ \vdots \\ CA^{n-1} \end{bmatrix}.

The system is observable if \text{rank}(\mathcal{O}(C, A)) = n.

Example: For the safety subspace (T, pH, a_w, M) with sensors measuring T, pH, and a_w, the observability matrix depends on the coupling between states in the A matrix. If M is not directly measured, it must be inferred from the dynamics. The linearized A matrix is:

A = \begin{bmatrix}
\frac{\partial F_T}{\partial T} & \frac{\partial F_T}{\partial pH} & \frac{\partial F_T}{\partial a_w} & \frac{\partial F_T}{\partial M} \\
\frac{\partial F_{pH}}{\partial T} & \frac{\partial F_{pH}}{\partial pH} & \frac{\partial F_{pH}}{\partial a_w} & \frac{\partial F_{pH}}{\partial M} \\
\frac{\partial F_{a_w}}{\partial T} & \frac{\partial F_{a_w}}{\partial pH} & \frac{\partial F_{a_w}}{\partial a_w} & \frac{\partial F_{a_w}}{\partial M} \\
\frac{\partial F_M}{\partial T} & \frac{\partial F_M}{\partial pH} & \frac{\partial F_M}{\partial a_w} & \frac{\partial F_M}{\partial M}
\end{bmatrix}.

The cross-coupling terms (e.g., \partial F_M / \partial T) determine whether M is observable from the available sensors.

L.2 Nonlinear Observability

For the nonlinear system, local weak observability can be checked via the Lie derivative rank condition. Define the observation space \mathcal{O}_S as the smallest vector space containing the measurement functions h_1, \ldots, h_p and closed under Lie differentiation along the drift vector field f. The system is locally weakly observable if:

\dim(d \mathcal{O}_S) = n.

For the food system, the measurement functions are h_1 = T, h_2 = pH, h_3 = a_w. The Lie derivatives L_f h_i, L_f^2 h_i, \ldots involve the drift terms, which couple the states. If the rank condition holds, the system is observable.


